---
import Layout from '@layouts/Layout.astro';
import '@styles/practice.css';
const repoUrl = 'https://github.com/Karan-S-Mittal/karan-s-mittal.github.io';
---
<Layout title="Building an Inspectable Engineering Publication | Karan Mittal" description="An implementation study of Karan Mittal’s Explanatory Systems Studio: interactive explanations, shared components, and verification.">
  <main class="practice-page">
    <span class="register">Implementation study / Independent project</span><h1>Make the explanation something a reader can test.</h1><p class="lead">Explanatory Systems Studio brings technical writing and interactive models into one publication. The regression explorer is a concrete example: readers can change the data and inspect the fit.</p>
    <div class="reading">
      <section aria-labelledby="problem"><h2 id="problem">The problem</h2><p>An equation, a chart, and a verbal explanation each reveal a different part of an idea. When they appear separately, the reader has to make the connections mentally. The design question was how to let a reader change an input and see those representations respond together.</p></section>
      <section aria-labelledby="implementation"><h2 id="implementation">The implementation</h2><p>The <a href="/blog/linear-regression-ordinary-least-squares/">linear-regression essay</a> includes an explorer with editable data points, residual geometry, and a loss plot. It is embedded in a shared exhibit component, which gives the interaction a title and explanatory caption.</p><p>The explorer loads as a React island when it becomes visible. The surrounding publication uses Astro’s static output. These boundaries are inspectable in the <a href="/visuals/">visuals gallery</a> and the <a href={repoUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">repository source</a>.</p><p>Shared components and visual tokens provide a common structure across essays. The <a href="/visuals/">visuals gallery</a> makes those architectural primitives inspectable as standalone assets.</p></section>
      <section aria-labelledby="verification"><h2 id="verification">How the implementation is checked</h2><p>The preflight suite checks source files for visual-language violations, missing exhibit information, and token issues. The automated browser suite covers page layout, image loading, navigation, and theme behaviour.</p><p>These checks establish specific implementation properties. They do not measure whether the explanation improves learning; that would require evidence from readers.</p></section>
      <section aria-labelledby="result"><h2 id="result">What a visitor can inspect</h2><p>The public result is a working explorer, a reusable exhibit structure, and source code that records the implementation. Every visual primitive can be tested live or downloaded.</p><div class="actions"><a class="action" href="/blog/linear-regression-ordinary-least-squares/">Try the explorer →</a><a class="action" href="/visuals/">Inspect visuals gallery →</a><a href={repoUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub repository ↗</a></div></section>
      <section aria-labelledby="tradeoffs"><h2 id="tradeoffs">The trade-off</h2><p>Connecting multiple interactive representations adds complexity to an otherwise static publication. The interaction has to earn that cost by helping a reader investigate the mechanism. Other explanations can stay as prose and diagrams.</p><p>The operating principle carries into client work: decide what the system needs to accomplish, make the important behaviour visible, and choose checks that correspond to the requirements.</p></section>
    </div>
    <section class="section closing"><h2>Need to make a difficult system understandable?</h2><p class="lead">I work with teams on system diagnosis, implementation, and the understanding needed to operate what they build.</p><div class="actions"><a class="action" href="/contact">Discuss your problem →</a><a href="/work">More work & engagement options →</a></div></section>
  </main>
</Layout>
